# Shell scripts must stay LF even when checked out on Windows (autocrlf) — a CR after
# the shebang breaks `#!/usr/bin/env bash` on Linux/CI (build_cloudflare.sh, copy-in script).
*.sh text eol=lf

# VB6 source is CRLF by format convention: VB6 wrote it that way, HexIDE writes it that way, and
# SerializationCorpusTests compares bytes. Without these rules a Linux/macOS checkout normalizes the
# demo projects to LF, so every demo module fails the round-trip gate on CI while passing on a
# Windows dev box (where core.autocrlf=true restores CRLF). Blobs stay LF in the repo — this only
# pins what lands in the working tree.
*.vbp text eol=crlf
*.vbg text eol=crlf
*.frm text eol=crlf
*.ctl text eol=crlf
*.pag text eol=crlf
*.cls text eol=crlf
*.bas text eol=crlf
*.dsr text eol=crlf

# Companion binaries carry embedded assets (icons, pictures, list contents). Line-ending
# normalization would corrupt them. None are tracked today; this is a guard for when a demo
# project that has one is added.
*.frx binary
*.ctx binary
*.pgx binary

# The VB6 conformance corpus under /corpus is measured data, not source. Whitespace in it is
# load-bearing — trailing spaces and tabs after a line-continuation underscore ARE several of the
# cases — so keep git's hands off the bytes. Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored `corpus/**`
# would match a directory of that name at any depth, which is the kind of over-broad rule that breaks
# things far away from where it was written.
/corpus/** -text
